window.docContents[19].resolve({"/Algebraic-Graph-Theory/#section-translations-and-coordinate-permutations":{"contents":"Translation by a fixed vector t is an automorphism of the Hamming graph: it\npreserves the Hamming distance since (t + x) - (t + y) = x - y. Under the\nisomorphism hypercubeIsoHamming, this corresponds to the automorphism\nhypercubeTranslate of Q[n] from\nVertex Transitivity Of Hypercubes. There we had to define xor on\nHypercubeVert n and prove its basic properties (such as HypercubeVert.xor_xor) by\nhand. On Fin n → Bool, addition is pointwise xor, and the group structure\n(associativity, 0, negation) comes for free from Mathlib. Only the\ncharacteristic-two simp lemmas above (x + x = 0 and its consequences) needed\nproofs, each a line or two, since Mathlib has no BooleanRing instance on\nFin n → Bool.\n\n/-- Translation by t (pointwise xor) as an automorphism of the Hamming graph. -/\ndef hammingTranslate {n : ℕ} (t : Fin n → Bool) :\n    hypercubeHamming n ≃g hypercubeHamming n where\n  toEquiv := Equiv.addLeft t\n  map_rel_iff' := by\n    intro x y\n    grind [Equiv.coe_addLeft, hypercubeHamming_adj, hammingDist_eq_hammingNorm]\n\n\n@[simp]\nlemma hammingTranslate_apply {n : ℕ} (t x : Fin n → Bool) :\n    hammingTranslate t x = t + x := rfl\n\n\nPermuting the coordinates by σ : Equiv.Perm (Fin n) also preserves the Hamming\ndistance, and hence is an automorphism of the Hamming graph.\n\nlemma hammingDist_comp_perm {ι : Type*} [Fintype ι] {β : Type*}\n    [DecidableEq β] (σ : Equiv.Perm ι) (x y : ι → β) :\n    hammingDist (x ∘ σ) (y ∘ σ) = hammingDist x y := Finset.card_equiv σ (by simp)\n\n\n/-- Permutation of coordinates by σ as an automorphism of the Hamming graph;\nx is sent to x ∘ σ.symm so that the i-th coordinate of the image is the\nσ⁻¹ i-th coordinate of x. -/\ndef hammingPermute {n : ℕ} (σ : Equiv.Perm (Fin n)) :\n    hypercubeHamming n ≃g hypercubeHamming n where\n  toEquiv := σ.arrowCongr (Equiv.refl Bool)\n  map_rel_iff' {x y} := by\n    rw [hypercubeHamming_adj, hypercubeHamming_adj,\n      ← hammingDist_comp_perm σ.symm x y]\n    rfl\n\n\n@[simp]\nlemma hammingPermute_apply {n : ℕ} (σ : Equiv.Perm (Fin n)) (x : Fin n → Bool) :\n    hammingPermute σ x = x ∘ σ.symm := rfl\n\n\nIn the automorphism group the inverses of translations and coordinate permutations\nare computed by symm; in particular translation is an involution (characteristic\ntwo again!).\n\n@[simp]\nlemma hammingTranslate_inv_apply {n : ℕ} (t x : Fin n → Bool) :\n    (hammingTranslate t)⁻¹ x = t + x := rfl\n\n\n@[simp]\nlemma hammingPermute_inv_apply {n : ℕ} (σ : Equiv.Perm (Fin n)) (x : Fin n → Bool) :\n    (hammingPermute σ)⁻¹ x = x ∘ σ := rfl\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Algebraic Graph Theory\u0009The Automorphism Group of the Hypercube*","header":"12.6.1. Translations and Coordinate Permutations","id":"/Algebraic-Graph-Theory/#section-translations-and-coordinate-permutations"},"/Subgraphs/#chapter-subgraphs":{"contents":"namespace SimpleGraph\nopen Subgraph\nsection basics\nvariable {V : Type*} {G : SimpleGraph V} {H : G.Subgraph}\n\n\nA subgraph of a graph G is a graph H with V(H) ⊆ V(G) and E(H) ⊆ E(G).\n\nWe have already seen the subgraph order G ≤ H on SimpleGraph V, which describes\nsubgraphs with the same vertex set (or more precisely the same vertex type). For a\ngeneral subgraph we need to allow the vertex set to vary. Mathlib formalizes this\nwith the type SimpleGraph.Subgraph.\n\nIn this chapter we introduce SimpleGraph.Subgraph and the bounded lattice structure\nof subgraphs. We then look at ways of constructing subgraphs: from vertices, edges\nand walks, by taking induced subgraphs, and by deleting vertices and edges. Since a\nSubgraph is not a SimpleGraph we next consider the ways to convert between the\ntwo (Subgraph.coe, Subgraph.spanningCoe and toSubgraph), spanning subgraphs,\nand the parts of the SimpleGraph API that have to be duplicated for Subgraph.\nSince we now have four different ways of talking about subgraphs, we compare them and\nsay when to use each. As a first application we use the De Bruijn–Erdős theorem to\nextend our greedy colouring bound to infinite graphs. We then introduce maps and\ncomaps of subgraphs, with subgraphs of the Mycielskian as a worked example.\n\nMatchings are subgraphs, so we next define the matching number ν G of a graph,\ncompare it with the vertex cover number τ G (weak duality ν G ≤ τ G), and\ncompute it for the hypercube and the Mycielskian.\n\nThe final section is more advanced: we introduce partial colourings, colourings\nthat need only be valid on a given set of vertices. These are the main tool in our\nproof of Brooks' theorem in Brooks Theorem.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean","header":"9. Subgraphs","id":"/Subgraphs/#chapter-subgraphs"},"/Subgraphs/#section-maps-and-comaps":{"contents":"Subgraph.map is a pushforward operation: given a graph homomorphism f : G →g K,\nbetween G : SimpleGraph V and K : SimpleGraph W, and a subgraph H : G.Subgraph,\nH.map f is the subgraph of K whose vertex set is f '' H.verts and whose edges\nare the images f u — f v of edges u — v of H. Note the contrast with\nSimpleGraph.map (Maps), which takes a plain function: here f\nmust be a homomorphism, since the image has to be a subgraph of the given graph K.\n\n#source Subgraph.map\n\n\nThere is also Subgraph.comap which is a pullback operation: given a homomorphism\nf : G →g K and a subgraph H of K, H.comap f is the subgraph of G whose\nvertex set is the preimage f⁻¹' H.verts and whose edges are those edges u — v of\nG for which f u — f v is an edge of H.\n\n#source Subgraph.comap\n\n\nAs for simple graphs (Maps), map f and comap f form a Galois\nconnection. Here this holds for every homomorphism f, not just for embeddings: a\nhomomorphism never sends two adjacent vertices to the same vertex, so no edge is\nlost when we map forwards.\n\n#check Subgraph.map_le_iff_le_comap -- H.map f ≤ H' ↔ H ≤ H'.comap f\n\n\nWe have Subgraph.inclusion which gives the graph homomorphism between two subgraphs\nH ≤ K (coerced to SimpleGraph on their respective vertex sets).\n\n#check Subgraph.inclusion -- {H K : G.Subgraph} (h : H ≤ K) : H.coe →g K.coe\n\n\nWe also have Subgraph.hom which gives the graph homomorphism from a subgraph H to\nthe original graph G.\n\n#check Subgraph.hom -- .. (H : G.Subgraph) : H.coe →g G\n\n\nCombining Subgraph.map with Subgraph.hom gives coeSubgraph which allows us to\ntake a subgraph of a subgraph coerced to SimpleGraph and get a subgraph of the\noriginal graph.\n\n#source Subgraph.coeSubgraph --  G'.coe.Subgraph → G.Subgraph\n\n\nexample {A : G.Subgraph} (B : A.coe.Subgraph) (v w : V) : (A.coeSubgraph B).Adj v w\n    ↔ ∃ (hv : v ∈ A.verts) (hw : w ∈ A.verts), B.Adj ⟨_, hv⟩ ⟨_, hw⟩ :=\n  Subgraph.coeSubgraph_adj B v w\n\n\nCombining Subgraph.comap with Subgraph.hom gives Subgraph.restrict: given\nsubgraphs A and B of G, A.restrict B is the part of B that lies in A, as\na subgraph of A.coe (i.e. with vertex type A.verts).\n\n#source Subgraph.restrict\n\n\nVertices of A are adjacent in A.restrict B iff they are adjacent in both A and\nB.\n\nexample {A B : G.Subgraph} (v w : A.verts) :\n    (A.restrict B).Adj v w ↔ A.Adj v w ∧ B.Adj v w := Iff.rfl\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Subgraphs","header":"9.4. Maps and Comaps","id":"/Subgraphs/#section-maps-and-comaps"},"/Walks/#section-hamiltonian-graphs-ore-dirac-and-bondy-chv-tal":{"contents":"In this section we prove three classical sufficient conditions for a graph to be\nHamiltonian. The oldest two are Ore's theorem, which says that a finite graph on\nat least 3 vertices is Hamiltonian if the sum of the degrees of every pair of\nnon-adjacent vertices is at least the number of vertices in the graph, and its\nimmediate (but earlier) corollary Dirac's theorem (minimum degree at least half\nthe number of vertices suffices).\n\nRather than prove Ore's theorem directly we prove the Bondy–Chvátal theorem: a\ngraph is Hamiltonian iff its closure is, where the closure is obtained by\nrepeatedly joining non-adjacent vertices whose degree sum is at least the number of\nvertices. Ore's theorem falls out immediately, since the closure of an Ore graph is\ncomplete and complete graphs are Hamiltonian (top_isHamiltonian).\n\nThe proof has four steps.\n\n1. A maximal path (one that cannot be extended at either end) whose endpoints\n   satisfy Ore's degree condition can be closed up into a cycle through exactly the\n   same vertices. The tools are Pósa rotations and a counting argument.2. Cutting a Hamiltonian cycle open at an edge and closing it up again by step 1\n   gives the one-step lemma: joining two non-adjacent vertices whose degree sum is\n   at least |V| does not change whether the graph is Hamiltonian.3. Repeating the one-step lemma, in rounds, shows that a graph is Hamiltonian iff\n   its closure is.4. Ore's and Dirac's theorems follow, since the closure of an Ore graph is complete.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Walks","header":"8.8. Hamiltonian Graphs: Ore, Dirac and Bondy–Chvátal*","id":"/Walks/#section-hamiltonian-graphs-ore-dirac-and-bondy-chv-tal"}});